response:
 It looks like your `generateRandomPlanets` function is trying to place new planets randomly within a 3D space, but it's checking for collisions only along one dimension at a time (x, y, or z). To improve the chances of finding non-overlapping positions, you can check for collisions in all three dimensions using the Euclidean distance formula. Here's an updated version of your `generateRandomPlanets` function:

```typescript
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets, minDistance) => {
  const planets = []
  let attempts = 0

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    }

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    let tooClose = false
    for (let i = 0; i < planets.length; i++) {
      const existingPlanet = planets[i]
      const distance = Math.sqrt(
        Math.pow(existingPlanet.position[0] - planet.position[0], 2) +
          Math.pow(existingPlanet.position[1] - planet.position[1], 2) +
          Math.pow(existingPlanet.position[2] - planet.position[2], 2)
      )

      if (distance < minDistance) {
        tooClose = true
        break
      }
    }

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet)
    }
  }

  return planets
}
```

This updated function uses a nested loop to calculate the Euclidean distance between the new planet and each existing planet, and checks if the distance is less than the minimum allowed distance. If so, it continues generating new planets.

 I'd be happy to help you write a React utility to remove widows in your text components! Widows refer to the last line of a paragraph that is left-justified and consists of only one word or a short phrase, which can sometimes look visually unappealing.

