response:
 The given code is a good starting point, but it assumes that there is a tragedy in the database with the exact letters "hystrio" as its name. In reality, we need to find all possible permutations of the given string "hystrio" and check each one against the database. This would be computationally expensive, especially for longer strings.
Instead, we can use a pre-computed list of tragic names and their summaries, and check if any of them match the given string. Here's how you can modify the code to achieve this:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;
// Pre-computed map of tragedy names and summaries
unordered_map<string, string> tragedies = {
    {"history", "An account of past events leading up to a particular result."},
    {"isthisory", "A tragic play by W.B. Yeats about a doomed love affair."},
    {"historion", "A person who acts on the stage or recites verses in public."},
    // Add more tragedies here...
};
int main() {
    string letters = "hystrio";
    string name;
    string summary;
    for (auto& tragedy : tragedies) {
        if (tragedy.first == letters) {
            name = tragedy.first;
            summary = tragedy.second;
            break;
        }
